+
+## Serial automaton export
+
+`ser --export-raw-automaton --no-viz program.ser` writes `ser-raw-v2` to the
+same output names. This opt-in representation bypasses Kleene elimination and
+semilinear expansion. `--export-raw` continues to produce the original v1
+representation; the two flags cannot be combined.
+
+The net and completion condition have the same semantics as v1. The target kind
+is `completed-outside-automaton`, and `excluded_automaton` replaces
+`excluded_semilinear`:
+
+- `states`: number of serial global states; state IDs are zero-based.
+- `initial`: initial global state ID.
+- `accepting`: accepting state IDs. Every global state accepts because a serial
+  history may stop after any completed request, including the empty history.
+- `edges`: objects `{source, target, response}`; each edge emits one response
+  token. `response` is an original Petri-net place ID, not an alphabet index.
+
+The excluded set is exactly the Parikh image of all accepted paths in this
+automaton. Target membership requires completion and nonmembership of the
+response vector in that Parikh image. It does not require matching the
+concurrent execution's final global state. States and edges are sorted and
+edges deduplicated for deterministic serialization. No serial states are
+eliminated and no solver is invoked.
+
+This representation avoids exponential semilinear subset expansion. Semantic
+translation and serial-automaton construction can still exceed resource
+limits; target membership can also be expensive. A resource limit must remain
+unknown, never count as nonmembership.
+
+Reproduce this incremental frontend change after the v1 patch with
+`scripts/setup-raw-automaton.sh`. Its patch is
+`scripts/ser-raw-automaton-export.patch`; it leaves the v1 export code intact.
